description:
Thank you Gerry Brevoort for allowing visitors, 3rd state record;

first noticed about a week before this;

update from Gerry Brevoort on Jan 21, 2025 "This is Day #91 of the Anna's Hummingbird in our yard...and it appears to be the final day. He was not seen after 8:25 a.m., following a morning of frantic feeding while hovering. It was a frigid 6 degrees with a windchill of -6. He came to the feeder at his usual time, but never perched on the feeder rail. Instead he fed 5 times in 50 minutes, always while hovering above the feeder. We put out the additional "upright" feeder with fresh nectar, hoping it might be a bit warmer for him, at least for a short while until it would freeze. He fed from both feeders and hovered and flew around the entire feeder area. He never perched while in our view. He took a last sip at 8:25 a.m. and then flew up and off to the west. We checked for him in his usual perching spots in my neighbor's yard (the rose trellis and the Viburnum) and throughout my garden area, to no avail. We watched for him for 4 more hours, and then return 4 hours later to check again -- no sign of the Anna's Hummingbird and we are concluding that he has left our yard. He appeared to be healthy, active, good color, alert, preening & feeding, before he departed. Temperature by evening was 7 degrees with a windchill of -10. Forecast is for us to experience a polar vortex with predicted temps tomorrow of -6/windchill of -8. So honored to have hosted this winged wonder and so humbled by his tenacity, resilience and beauty!"
